---
import Film from '../../plate/Film.astro';
import { renderFormula } from '../../../lib/formulas/render';
import { CUES, TIMELINE } from './story';
import './style.css';

const formula = (tex: string) => renderFormula(tex, false);

const labels = [
  { id: 'bond-pure', tex: String.raw`h=\mathbf S_j\cdot\mathbf S_{j+1}`, blue: false },
  { id: 'bond-aklt', tex: String.raw`h+\tfrac13h^2`, blue: false },
  { id: 'floor60', tex: String.raw`\tfrac4{105}\log\tfrac{80}{79}`, blue: true },
  { id: 'floor2304', tex: String.raw`\tfrac{\log20}{784}`, blue: true },
  { id: 'floor-open', tex: String.raw`\tfrac{\log10}{392}`, blue: true },
  { id: 'purity-lemma', tex: String.raw`1-P'\le\frac{u^2}{2(1-u)^2}`, blue: false },
  { id: 'purity-gap', tex: String.raw`\frac1b\log\frac{T}{1-T}`, blue: true },
  { id: 'partition', tex: 'Z_n(b)', blue: false },
  { id: 'transfer', tex: 'X_b', blue: false },
  { id: 'physical-purity', tex: String.raw`T=\frac{Z_n(2b)}{Z_n(b)^2}`, blue: false },
  { id: 'spatial-purity', tex: String.raw`S=\frac{Z_{2n}(b)}{Z_n(b)^2}`, blue: false },
  { id: 'defect', tex: String.raw`C^{-1}r^{2^j}`, blue: true },
  { id: 'b', tex: 'b', blue: false },
  { id: 'step-weights', tex: String.raw`\lvert\lambda_i\rvert^n`, blue: false },
];
---

<Film
  tag="uruk-gap-ladder"
  cues={CUES}
  timeline={TIMELINE}
  description="A film of a ring of quantum magnets and its energy ladder. Computed gaps for small rings are compared with three claimed lower bounds. The proof is illustrated through two probability distributions and repeated doubling. A complete text transcript follows."
  caption="A ring of quantum magnets and its ladder of energies; the gap, computed for small rings, closing for spin one half and levelling off for spin one; and the claimed proof that it never closes for spin one, which squares two distributions hidden in one number."
>
  <Fragment slot="labels">
    {labels.map((label) => <span class:list={['film-label', 'film-label--small', { 'film-label--blue': label.blue }]} data-label={label.id}><span set:html={formula(label.tex)} /></span>)}
    <span class="film-label film-label--stamp" data-label="status"><span>CLAIMED · UNDER REVIEW</span></span>
  </Fragment>
  <p class="film-method">How the film is made. The plotted small-ring gaps were computed by Lanczos iteration for spin one half at lengths 4 through 20 and spin one at lengths 4 through 14. The spin-one ring of eight has 6,561 states. Its complete diagonalization, grouped into 683 distinct levels, supplies the energy ladder, degeneracies, energy weights and purities. Intermediate ladder positions carry the object between computed rings; they are not additional spectral calculations. The dashed 0.41050 line is White and Huse's 1993 numerical estimate, cited by the article.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The needles illustrate quantum spins; they are not classical directions or a computed quantum state. The AKLT halves and pairs picture its valence-bond construction. The sheet, bond-history marks, transfer cursor and step weights are schematic. The film does not compute the eigenvalues of the spatial transfer operator. Its energy weights are computed from all 6,561 states and normalized at each inverse temperature b. Raising b cools the system. The bar for all other states groups their weight for display; the purity still uses the separate states.</p>
  <p class="film-method">Squaring and renormalizing physical energy weights doubles b exactly. The physical and spatial purities share the same partition functions, which is why the proof can couple doubling in both directions. The film uses the paper's exact starting sequence, while leaving the uncomputed constants C and r as letters.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The gap-from-purity illustration is an elementary consequence of the weights. The largest weight is at least the purity T, and every other weight is at most 1 − T. Thus T &gt; 1/2 forces a unique lowest state and gives <span set:html={formula(String.raw`\gamma\ge\frac1b\log\frac{T}{1-T}`)} />. On the ring of eight at b = 16 the bound is about 0.482, below its computed gap of about 0.594. This illustrates the argument; it is not the paper's uniform lower bound. The blue steps are the papers' claimed bounds for their specified even rings and odd open chains with endpoint field 3/5. They do not claim the numerical value 0.41050.</p>
  <p class="film-method">Tasaki's review covers the analytic argument. The article records that he has not independently checked the computer-assisted estimates. The finite calculations used for the film do not validate those certificates or the proof for arbitrarily long rings.</p>
</Film>

<script>
  import './element';
</script>
